            "correct_answer": "$6e^{j(\\omega/12-\\pi/10)}$",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Multiply the magnitudes to get $2\\cdot 3=6$. Combine the phases: $-\\pi/5+\\pi/10=-\\pi/10$, and combine the $\\omega$ terms: $\\omega/4-\\omega/6=\\omega/12$. So $Y(j\\omega)=6e^{j(\\omega/12-\\pi/10)}$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
